# Rust wire protocols

See also: `languages/rust` (230) — base style + the "domain newtypes at boundaries" type idiom this
rule extends. `frameworks/api-surface-classes` (332) — the broader wire-format taxonomy this rule
applies to in Rust.

Applies to every (de)serialization boundary: FFI payloads, event-stream JSON, gRPC / Cap'n Proto /
MessagePack, HTTP request bodies, persisted artifacts. The rules are the same for all wire formats;
serde derives are the canonical example.

## Wire structs are not domain structs

A wire struct's job is bytes-on-the-wire stability. A domain struct's job is in-memory invariant
enforcement. Conflating them locks the wire format to whatever the domain happens to look like
today — every refactor becomes a breaking protocol change.

```rust
// Good — separate types, explicit conversion
mod domain {
    pub struct WorkflowPlan {
        pub id: WorkflowId,                      // newtype, validated
        pub phases: Vec<Phase>,                  // domain Phase enum
        pub budget: RetryBudget,                 // newtype with invariants
    }
}

mod protocol {
    #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
    pub struct WorkflowPlanV1 {
        pub id: Uuid,                            // raw uuid on the wire
        pub phases: Vec<PhaseV1>,                // wire-format Phase
        pub budget: u32,                         // raw u32 on the wire
    }
}

impl TryFrom<protocol::WorkflowPlanV1> for domain::WorkflowPlan {
    type Error = WorkflowPlanError;
    fn try_from(wire: protocol::WorkflowPlanV1) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> { /* validate */ }
}

// Bad — one type serves both surfaces; the wire is locked to the domain shape
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct WorkflowPlan {
    pub id: Uuid,
    pub phases: Vec<Phase>,
    pub budget: u32,
}
```

The exception: when stability semantics are genuinely identical — e.g. a primitive config struct
that's just a serde-derived bag of fields and is also the in-memory shape. In that case one type
is fine, but the moment you want to add a domain invariant (`budget > 0`, `phases` non-empty), the
types split.

Naming convention: domain types live in `domain/` or directly in their owning module. Wire types
live in `protocol/` (or `wire/`) and carry an explicit version suffix (`PlanV1`, `EnvelopeV2`).

## Version wire formats explicitly

Every wire format has an explicit version field. New consumers detect old payloads and old
consumers fail predictably on new ones.

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct EnvelopeV1<T> {
    pub version: ProtocolVersion,
    pub payload: T,
}

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(rename_all = "snake_case")]
pub enum ProtocolVersion {
    V1,
    V2,
}
```

Migration shape:

- Adding optional fields to an existing version is additive; mark them `#[serde(default,
  skip_serializing_if = "...")]`.
- Removing fields, reinterpreting fields, or changing field types is a new version. Bump the
  version enum, keep the old variant decodable, add a converter.
- Never silently drop unknown fields from versions newer than the consumer expects — log + reject,
  or pass-through if the consumer can tolerate it.

`#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]` is the right default for strictly-validated payloads (control-plane
commands). Open-set payloads (telemetry events, debug snapshots) can tolerate unknown fields with
explicit logging.

## Deserialization validates domain invariants

A wire struct deserializes into the wire type, not directly into the domain type. The conversion
from wire to domain is where invariants get checked. This is where `TryFrom` earns its keep.

```rust
// Good — wire round-trips serde; domain enforces invariants
mod protocol {
    #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
    pub struct WorkflowIdWire(pub String);
}

mod domain {
    pub struct WorkflowId(String);

    impl TryFrom<protocol::WorkflowIdWire> for WorkflowId {
        type Error = WorkflowIdError;
        fn try_from(wire: protocol::WorkflowIdWire) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> {
            validate_workflow_id(&wire.0)?;
            Ok(Self(wire.0))
        }
    }
}

// Bad — wire and domain are the same type; serde accepts anything that parses
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct WorkflowId(pub String);
```

For inline validation during deserialization (one-shot enums or value objects), implement a custom
`Deserialize` via the visitor pattern or use a typed-wrapper crate (`serde_with`, `garde`,
`validator`). The principle is the same: invariants are enforced before the domain type exists.

## Optional field discipline

Optional wire fields are tolerated; defensive matching at every read site is not. Push the optional
into the conversion layer:

```rust
// Good — conversion layer resolves the optional once
mod protocol {
    #[derive(Deserialize)]
    pub struct AgentReportV1 {
        pub agent_id: Uuid,
        pub status: String,
        pub metrics: Option<MetricsV1>,        // optional on the wire
    }
}

mod domain {
    pub struct AgentReport {
        pub agent_id: AgentId,
        pub status: AgentStatus,
        pub metrics: Metrics,                  // domain has a default
    }

    impl From<protocol::AgentReportV1> for AgentReport {
        fn from(wire: protocol::AgentReportV1) -> Self {
            AgentReport {
                agent_id: AgentId(wire.agent_id),
                status: wire.status.parse().unwrap_or(AgentStatus::Unknown),
                metrics: wire.metrics.map(Into::into).unwrap_or_default(),
            }
        }
    }
}
```

`Option<T>` in the domain type is reserved for cases where absence is genuinely meaningful (the
report covers a partial window; the field is N/A for this agent kind). Don't propagate every
"the wire might omit this" optional all the way through.

## Roundtrip + compatibility tests

Every wire format ships with roundtrip and compatibility tests. See `languages/rust` (230)
Testing section for the general principle; the protocol-specific cases:

- `encode(decode(x)) == x` — wire is fixed-point under round-trip.
- `decode(old_fixture) == expected_current_model` — old payloads continue to decode under the
  current consumer.
- Unknown fields are handled per the format's stated policy (rejected, ignored, or pass-through).
- Malformed bytes fail with a typed error, not a panic.

Fixtures live in `tests/fixtures/<protocol>/v<N>/` and are committed. A protocol change that
breaks a committed fixture is a deliberate version bump, not a silent regression.

## Backward-compatibility ratchet

Once a wire version is observed in production, that version's encoding is permanent. Adding
optional fields is allowed; changing the meaning of existing fields is not.

The audit trail (`foundations/result-handling`, event streams, evidence bundles) is especially
sensitive: a v1 evidence bundle written to durable storage must decode forever under any later
consumer, or the operator loses the ability to prove what the system did. Reinterpreting fields in
existing versions corrupts the audit trail silently.

When a version becomes obsolete, mark it deprecated in the code but keep the decoder; remove only
after a documented retention window plus a migration that re-encodes any stored payloads.

## What NOT to do

- Do not use one struct for both the wire format and the domain type unless stability semantics
  are demonstrably identical and you accept that domain refactors are wire-breaking changes.
- Do not omit the `version` field from wire envelopes — even the first version.
- Do not deserialize directly into the domain type without a validation hop.
- Do not propagate every wire-side `Option` all the way through the domain. Resolve optionals at
  the conversion layer.
- Do not change the encoding of an existing version after it has shipped. Bump.
- Do not assume `#[serde(default)]` is free — confirm the default value is semantically correct in
  every existing call path, not just convenient at the deserialization site.

